The period from 1656 to 1711 covers the fifty-five years between the [[Sinchew War]] and the [[First War of the Isthmus]]. It is the period in which the colony absorbed the western province of the former [[Hachinawa]] and in which its labour system reversed.

The population was the largest the colony had held: the Baiduist exiles and the Hahkina of the annexed province were governed together for the first time, and the colony's administration was occupied with the land they occupied. Baiduists moved from the plantation districts into the [[Hachinawa]] countryside, the cost of plantation labour rose with their departure, and the [[Secret Trade]] returned on a scale it had not reached since the 1630s.

The period ends with the edict of 1680 banning the importation of enslaved people, and with the approach of the war over the isthmus.

== Settlement and the province (1656–1662) ==

[[Ng Tek-seng]] remained governor after the [[Treaty of Tamsui (1656)|treaty]] and governed for a further six years. The colony's task in these years was the incorporation of the western province.

Kayatsi administered the province for his lifetime under the terms of the treaty. The assembly of Hachinawa was retained and continued to legislate within the province, subject to his veto, and the administration of the annexed territory was conducted by the institutions of the state that had surrendered it.

The colony's population was reorganised in the same years. Many of the Baiduist households of the plantation districts left them for the countryside of the former empire, where land was available and the provincial administration was not the colonial administration. The plantations lost labour at the time they were acquiring land, and the two movements together set the terms of the period.

== Legal dispossession (1662–1680) ==

Ng Tek-seng was succeeded in 1662 by Ông Chì-kong, whose administration was directed against the native population of the province.

The instrument was legal rather than military. The colonial administration encouraged Zong settlement of the province's land by adjudicating the claims of settlers and of Hahkina holders in a series of settlements unfavourable to the latter. The proceedings were regular: claims were brought, they were heard, and they were determined under a body of law that the colony had made. Land passed from Hahkina holders to Zong settlers without a campaign and without a confiscation that could be described as one.

The effect on the province was the transfer of its cultivable land within a generation. The assembly continued to sit and to legislate, and the legislation it made governed a population that was losing the ground it lived on.

The same years reduced the office of the provincial administrator. Kayatsi retained the office for his lifetime as the treaty provided, and the powers attached to it were narrowed by enactment rather than by removal: the competence of the assembly was defined so as to exclude the land settlements, the administrator's veto was confined to the matters the assembly was permitted to consider, and the provincial courts were brought within the jurisdiction of the colonial judiciary. Nothing in these measures revoked the treaty, and their cumulative effect was to place the province's land beyond the reach of its own institutions.

An edict of 1679 prohibited the migration of Hahkina out of the province. It was issued in the year after Kayatsi's death, under the administrator selected by the governor of Sinchew, and it bound the population of the province to its territory at the point at which their land was passing out of their hands. Land could be lost and the people who had held it could not leave: the effect was to keep the province's dispossessed within it, as labour for the same settlements that had taken their ground.

Kayatsi's authority over the province was accordingly at its greatest in 1656 and declined for the remainder of his life. He died in 1678 at the age of 68, having administered the province for twenty-two years and in the year in which the last of the settlements of his administration was concluded. Under the treaty his successor was selected by the governor of Sinchew, and the office passed from the king of the former empire to an administrator appointed by the colony.

=== Return of slavery ===

The labour system of the plantation districts reversed in this period.

The departure of Baiduist households for the province raised the cost of plantation labour, and the [[Secret Trade]] became profitable again as it did so. The trade had been conducted unlawfully since its foundation in 1572, and it had declined in the 1610s because the arrival of more than a million Baiduist exiles had made the employment of a labourer cheaper than the procurement of a captive. The same population moving out of the plantation districts restored the conditions in which enslavement paid.

The trade expanded through the middle of the century and reached in the 1670s a scale it had not held since before the Sinchew War. The enslaved population of the colony grew by importation for the first time in sixty years, and the traffic was carried on with the knowledge of the colonial administration, which was complicit in it.

== After the edict (1680–1711) ==

The [[Secret Trade]] ended in 1680. Diplomatic pressure from the [[Sultanate of Maros]] led the Zong court to deploy naval forces against the raiders, and an edict of that year prohibited the importation of enslaved people into the colony.

The edict closed the market and not the institution. Chattel slavery continued: the people already held remained held, their children were born into slavery, and no provision was made for emancipation. The enslaved population grew by natural increase thereafter, and the plantations continued to be worked by enslaved labour.

The raiders' smaller operations continued in secret for decades after the edict, and the traffic did not end at once. What ended in 1680 was the large-scale procurement that had supplied the colony since 1656.

=== Separatism ===

Opposition to the administration of the province took organised form in this period.

The [[Brotherhood of the Free Peoples]] was a separatist body in the former empire which worked for the independence of the province. It was aligned with [[Free Sinchew]], the state established north-east of [[Tamsui]] by the remnant of the Baiduist revolutionary army in 1656, and it organised on the model of a state that the war had created out of a defeated population.

The Brotherhood drew on the Hahkina of the province and on Baiduist settlers in it, and two objects were advanced within it.

The first was the restoration of an independent Hachinawa. Its advocates sought the province's separation from the colony and the re-establishment of the state that the treaty of 1656 had divided, and they looked to the Hahkina of the former empire as the people whose state it would be.

The second was a union of the repressed peoples of the region. Its advocates held that the Hahkina, the Baiduists and the other peoples subject to the colony had a common position, and that a polity of their own would have to include them all rather than restore a state in which the Baiduists had been settlers and the Hahkina its subjects.

The two objects were not reconciled, and the division persisted through the period. The first looked to a kingdom that had existed and the second to one that had not, and the colony's administration was able to deal with a body that contained both by treating its demands as a claim to restore the empire rather than as a general rising.

The Brotherhood's existence made the province a territory requiring garrison at the same time as the colony's frontier was being contested elsewhere.

=== Approach to the isthmus war ===

By the end of the period the colony held the largest territory and the largest population in its history, worked by an enslaved population that could no longer be added to, and administered over a province whose inhabitants had an organised movement for independence.

The [[First War of the Isthmus]] began in 1711. The colony's forces were engaged at [[Sant Cristòfor]] and on the Balboa frontier, and the reclamation of [[Free Sinchew]] was undertaken in the same war.

