The colony's population increased from about 1.5 million in 1655 to about 3.3 million in 1710. The whole of the increase in the first return is attributable to the annexation: the province's Hahkina appear in the returns of 1660 and had not been enumerated by the colony before. The population of the colony's own territory was about the same size in 1660 as in 1655.

The Tianist population doubled over the period, from 92,348 to 181,046, and grew faster after 1662 than before it. The class grew by Zong settlement of the annexed province, which the administration encouraged, and it is the only class whose increase the administration intended.

The Baiduist population increased steadily at about three-quarters of one per cent a year. Its growth is natural and not the result of immigration, and its distribution changed while its numbers rose: the households that left the plantation districts for the countryside of the former Hachinawa did not leave the colony, and they appear in the returns of the province rather than of the districts they had left. The effect of the movement is not visible in the series and is the cause of the change in the colony's labour system recorded under [[History of Sinchew (1656-1711)|the return of slavery]] above.

The Hahkina increased in every return, from 842,317 to 1,206,193. Their numbers are not the measure of their circumstances. The period was one in which their holdings passed to Zong settlers through legal settlement, and the class that lost the land is the class whose count rose: large numbers of Hahkina left the countryside for the towns of the province, where they settled as labourers and as a population without land of its own. The province was urbanised in these years: its towns received a population that had been dispersed over its cultivable ground. An edict of 1679 prohibited the migration of Hahkina out of the province, so that the movement was confined within its boundaries and the population that left the land did not leave the territory.

The enslaved population rose from 64,987 to 110,753 between 1655 and 1680, and from 110,753 to 118,829 in the thirty years after the edict. The change in slope is the [[Secret Trade]]'s: the class grew by importation while the trade ran and by natural increase after 1680. The edict prohibited the importation of enslaved people and did not emancipate any.

The free native population increased slowly throughout, from 48,619 to 64,573.

